Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?[daveey]=20lifecycle=20guard:=20keep=20$(?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=A6),=20$((=E2=80=A6))=20and=20backticks=20inside=20their?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20shell=20word?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The guard tokenizer (shlex with punctuation_chars ";&|()") cut a word at the
parentheses of a command or arithmetic substitution, so the rest of the
command fell into a separate segment without its program. Real bash ran
./r.sh (which restarts the gateway) while scan_gateway_lifecycle allowed:

  bash 2>$(echo /dev/null) < ./r.sh
  bash 2>/dev/$(echo null) < ./r.sh
  bash 2>&$((1+1)) < ./r.sh
  bash {a[$((1+1))]}>/dev/null < ./r.sh
  bash {a[`echo 1`]}>/dev/null < ./r.sh
  x=`echo 1` bash ./r.sh
  echo "$(bash ./r.sh)"  /  `bash /x/huge.sh`   (review repros)

New cron/lifecycle_guard_substitutions.py separates executable expansions
lexically: each $(…)/backtick span (and command substitutions nested in
$((…))) becomes a NUL-prefixed placeholder inside its word, and its body is
scanned as its own command text, recursively. The span matcher tracks
quotes, ANSI-C quotes, comments, heredoc bodies and case-pattern `)`, and is
bounded at 32 nesting levels (fail closed). Tokenization moves to
cron/lifecycle_guard_tokens.py, which also walks the original text, the
reading where an unquoted empty expansion vanishes (`bash $(true) < ./r.sh`),
and an ANSI-C-normalized form, so the change only adds segments.

Tests: real-bash regressions for each shape and the review repros
(test_lifecycle_guard_substitution_words/_adversarial/_bounds); the
generated heredoc shape corpus gains SUBSTITUTION_PREFIXES across every
redirect place x wrapper x nesting x target (incl. stdin), with the
main_verdicts fixture regenerated at 4dc17201a9.
